{
  "157": {
    "document_id": "157",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The document is merely a title/navigation stub ('Chair Small Business Committee on Mark Up on Venture Capital and Education bills') with no substantive content. There is no assertion or implication that the senator caused any government outcome or delivered benefit."
  },
  "189": {
    "document_id": "189",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Leahy's hearing statement advocates allowing cameras in federal courtrooms, notes he has cosponsored two pending bills, and criticizes the Bush administration's secrecy. Under negative clarification (1) this is pure position-taking and criticism of the administration, and under negative clarification (2) cosponsorship of pending legislation that has produced no benefit is not credit-claiming. No delivered benefit is claimed."
  },
  "744": {
    "document_id": "744",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Allard sent a letter requesting that the Air Force clarify permissible religious expression at the Academy. This is a request/advocacy effort with no achieved outcome, which negative clarification (2) excludes from credit-claiming."
  },
  "873": {
    "document_id": "873",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces that Medicare will waive the late-enrollment penalty for hurricane-affected seniors and explicitly ties that favorable administrative action to Landrieu's prior letter and the delegation's inquiries ('responds to urgent calls... made by U.S. Senator Mary L. Landrieu'; 'the inconsistency our delegation brought to its attention'). Under clarification (3), tying the senator's prior letter/appeal to a subsequent favorable executive action with an asserted causal link is credit-claiming."
  },
  "964": {
    "document_id": "964",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Landrieu and Kerry 'call for' local contracts in Katrina/Rita rebuilding and write the SBA to speed up loss verification. This is a request/advocacy effort with no achieved outcome, excluded by negative clarification (2). No benefit is claimed as delivered."
  },
  "1043": {
    "document_id": "1043",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces a $78,046 federal grant to an Ohio fire department 'through Sen. DeWine's bill,' states that DeWine 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' that he 'helped secure $650 million' for it, and touts quantified results ($73 million to 878 Ohio fire departments). This squarely fits clarification (2) ('secured' funding), (4) (authorship of enacted legislation delivering tangible benefits), and (5) (quantified results of the senator's legislative work)."
  },
  "1139": {
    "document_id": "1139",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Coleman's statement extends sympathies on the death of Rep. Norwood and honors his legacy. This is ceremonial content/condolence, excluded by negative clarification (4). No government benefit is claimed."
  },
  "1610": {
    "document_id": "1610",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement at the CHIP enrollment ceremony credits others (Baucus, Rockefeller, Grassley, Hatch) for the bipartisan bill and criticizes the President for threatening a veto. Reid claims no personal role in producing the outcome, and the bill has not yet been enacted into law (it is being sent to a President threatening rejection). Under negative clarifications (1) and (3), this is position-taking and criticism, not personal credit-claiming."
  },
  "1736": {
    "document_id": "1736",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the request for an expedited hearing on S.165 is itself unfulfilled advocacy, the release's final paragraph ties Domenici's earlier appeal to a favorable administrative outcome: 'Domenici and Representatives... asked Agriculture Secretary... and President... to approve any request from New Mexico for emergency aid,' followed by 'The USDA Farm Service Agency announced Friday that livestock producers in 20 New Mexico counties can qualify for low-interest emergency loans.' This juxtaposition asserts a causal link between the senator's request and a concrete benefit (emergency loan eligibility), which clarification (3) treats as credit-claiming."
  },
  "2368": {
    "document_id": "2368",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Craig announces a concrete home-state benefit (expansion of the Lewiston VA clinic to five days per week with added mental health services) and links it to his own advocacy: he thanks VA officials 'for their willingness to hear my concerns and - more importantly - respond to the needs of the veterans.' Under clarification (1), announcing a federal benefit to home-state constituents claims association with it, and the release implies his intervention helped cause the VA's decision (clarification 3)."
  },
  "2382": {
    "document_id": "2382",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Most of the release concerns newly introduced legislation (S.1588), which alone would not qualify (negative clarification 2). However, it also states: 'By intervening with the Department of Homeland Security and removing bureaucratic red tape, Sen. Landrieu recently ensured that Rayan, a little girl from Iraq with life-threatening birth defects, was able to come with her mother to the United States for treatment.' This is an explicit claim that the senator's intervention caused a concrete favorable outcome, satisfying clarification (3)'s causal-link criterion."
  },
  "2697": {
    "document_id": "2697",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye announces $225,000 in federal funding for coqui frog control on the Big Island and explicitly claims personal responsibility: 'I was disappointed when the... OMB denied Hawaii's request... I then went looking and found another federal funding source to at least begin the control and containment process.' This is a first-person claim of securing federal dollars for his state, the paradigmatic form of credit-claiming under clarifications (1) and (2)."
  },
  "2827": {
    "document_id": "2827",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a National Review op-ed by Lieberman on the war in Iraq and the struggle against 'Islamist extremism.' It is pure position-taking on a national security issue with no claim of any delivered benefit, excluded by negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "2895": {
    "document_id": "2895",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage of the Improving America's Security Act and states 'The bill also contains McCaskill's amendment to provide whistleblower protection and collective bargaining rights for airport screeners,' and that 'Missouri stands to receive an estimated $26.5 million in homeland security funds.' Claiming authorship/inclusion of her own provision in legislation that has passed at least one chamber, delivering benefits to identifiable beneficiaries, fits clarification (4)."
  },
  "3038": {
    "document_id": "3038",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Snowe's statement praises Vice Admiral Allen's qualifications and expresses confidence in him as the nominee for Coast Guard Commandant. This is praise of a nominee, excluded by negative clarification (5). She claims no personal role in any delivered benefit; her subcommittee chairmanship is general position/leadership reference, not tied to a concrete outcome (negative clarification 7)."
  },
  "3143": {
    "document_id": "3143",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage (79-14) of the Farm Bill and states 'Graham worked to include additional grant programs that could benefit research and training being conducted in South Carolina' and that the bill 'includes a Graham backed provision that would provide economic adjustment assistance... to textile manufacturers.' These are claims of authorship/inclusion of the senator's own provisions in legislation that has passed at least one chamber, benefiting identifiable beneficiaries in his state, per clarification (4)."
  },
  "3149": {
    "document_id": "3149",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage (52-40) of the FY2008 budget resolution and states it includes 'a provision championed by Reed to help establish an affordable housing fund' for low-income families. Claiming authorship of his own provision in legislation that has passed at least one chamber, delivering tangible benefits to identifiable beneficiaries, fits clarification (4). Some ambiguity exists because a budget resolution is a blueprint rather than final appropriation, but the explicit 'championed by Reed' authorship claim for a provision in passed legislation satisfies the codebook's criterion."
  },
  "3817": {
    "document_id": "3817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sessions welcomes/applauds TVA's decision to proceed with the Bellefonte nuclear application ('I am pleased that TVA's Board of Directors voted...'; 'I applaud the Board'). Saying the issue 'has been a priority of mine' claims interest, not causation of the decision. Under negative clarification (6), welcoming or applauding an outcome without claiming a personal role in causing it is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "4328": {
    "document_id": "4328",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Alexander and Corker announce a favorable Department of Transportation decision providing Essential Air Service between Jackson, TN and Cincinnati, and the release ties it to their own action: 'Senator Alexander and Senator Corker wrote a letter in support of Jackson's application to Department of Transportation Secretary Mary Peters.' Under clarification (3), tying the senators' prior letter/appeal to a subsequent favorable administrative action with an asserted causal link is credit-claiming; it also fits clarification (1) as an announcement of a federal benefit to home-state constituents."
  },
  "4403": {
    "document_id": "4403",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release primarily announces introduction of the R-HoPE Act (which alone would not qualify, per negative clarification 2). However, it explicitly claims that provisions in current law were authored by Domenici: the Medicare geographic-adjustment provisions were 'included in the MMA that Domenici first authored and offered as legislation in February 2003' and are now delivering higher Medicare reimbursements to rural physicians in New Mexico. This is a claim of authorship of the senator's own provisions in enacted legislation delivering tangible benefits to identifiable beneficiaries, which fits clarification (4). The difficulty is that the release mixes an unpassed bill with a retrospective authorship claim; the latter tips the classification to credit-claiming."
  }
}
